{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Added a Pydantic-ecosystem comparison and end-to-end environment traces. PydanticAI supports API-key shortcuts but also direct injection of official OpenAI, Anthropic, and boto3 clients; Rebar should use client injection so Pydantic validates only the non-secret operation snapshot while the official SDK owns auth. FastAPI/Pydantic Settings and Instructor confirm the common environment-at-process-boundary convention. Local: shell or external tooling exports the provider variable; the Rebar-owned SDK factory consumes the native environment chain and injects the client into PydanticAI. GitHub Actions: the workflow maps a GitHub secret only onto the Rebar step environment; the same SDK path runs, no GitHub Secrets API or raw CLI secret is involved, and missing fork/Dependabot secrets fail closed. AWS SSM Parameter Store: ECS or deployment bootstrap maps SecureString to the provider env variable using the task execution role; Rebar does not call SSM. Bedrock instead uses the task role through boto3 with no stored API key. AWS confirms ECS environment injection is start-time only, so rotation requires a new task/forced deployment. A host application may alternatively retrieve SSM and inject a constructed client, retaining retrieval/rotation ownership. Gitleaks found no leaks after recording the research."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786936863532779001,"uuid":"7d7ac230-b401-4a68-aa71-b653943a3d47"}